# 2. THE EXACT VISUAL DIRECTION

The visual direction is extremely important.

I want a **minimalist black-and-white board and interface with naturally colored photographs of real places**.

This does not mean making every image black and white.

The distinction is:

- Board: predominantly black, white, and neutral tones.
- UI: predominantly black, white, and neutral tones.
- Landmark photographs: genuine photographs in their natural colors.
- Player identities: restrained colors or distinct symbols where needed.
- Ownership: clear, understated visual indicators.
- Gameplay highlights: minimal, purposeful, and easy to understand.

The game must feel like a carefully art-directed premium board game, combining editorial design, architectural photography, and a refined physical-board aesthetic.

Aim for a design that feels intentional, calm, distinctive, and expensive without unnecessary decoration.

## 2.1 Color palette

Use a restrained palette built around:

- Deep black or charcoal for dark surfaces.
- Clean white or warm white for the board.
- Neutral gray for secondary information.
- Fine dark borders and subtle dividers.
- Small, consistent player-specific accents.
- Natural colors in actual photographs.

Avoid:
- Neon colors.
- Purple AI-style gradients.
- Rainbow gradients.
- Artificial radial glows.
- Excessive glassmorphism.
- Glossy, oversized dashboard cards.
- Candy-colored badges.
- Decorative progress bars.
- Excessive rounded pills.
- Large colored header strips.
- Unnecessary shadows.
- Decorative animations that compete with the board.

Do not introduce a new design language on every screen.

The title screen, board, property dossier, action controls, dialogs, settings, player profiles, and end-game screen must look like parts of the same product.

## 2.2 Typography

Choose a consistent, highly readable type system.

Use:
- Clear sans-serif typography for interface text.
- A restrained monospace style for monetary values when appropriate.
- Consistent type sizes and line heights.
- Strong contrast between headings, labels, and secondary information.
- Tabular numerals for balances and prices where supported.

Do not use tiny typography to force excessive information into a small area.

Do not use excessive letter spacing.

Do not use uppercase text everywhere.

Do not truncate important words.

Every screen should have an obvious visual hierarchy.

## 2.3 Avoid generic AI-generated design

Do not make the game look like an automatically assembled AI dashboard.

Avoid:
- Repeated cards inside cards.
- Excessive decorative borders.
- Unnecessary section headings.
- Repeated labels that communicate the same information.
- Fake analytical charts.
- Decorative statistics that do not support gameplay.
- Glowing panels surrounding ordinary text.
- Excessive badges and icon combinations.
- Huge empty hero sections during active gameplay.
- Overdesigned controls that obscure the board.

Use whitespace deliberately.

Every visual element must help the player understand the game, navigate the interface, or enjoy the experience.

